# keur secret control — private-key / keystore FILE committed by name.
#
# Why this exists: the #1..#3 "deadly files" in every accidental-commit guide are
# .env (covered by SEC-100/KEUR-SECRET-003), cloud credential files, and SSH /
# private keys. KEUR-SEC-001 catches private-key *content* (the BEGIN line), but a
# key file can slip in whose content isn't scanned (binary keystore, or a name
# that screams "key" the author renamed). This rule blocks the well-known key /
# keystore FILENAMES outright — a committed private key is a house-key handed out
# at a train station.
#
# BLOCKS: a committed private key/keystore is a definite credential exposure.
#
# @see KEUR-SEC-001 (content-based sibling), and the "never commit" guides
#      (id_rsa, *.pem, *.key, *.p12/.pfx/.jks, credentials.csv, service-account).
id: KEUR-SECRET-010
title: Private key / keystore / cloud-credential file committed
category: secret
severity: critical
engine: file-presence
action: block
target:
  filenames: id_rsa id_dsa id_ecdsa id_ed25519 credentials.csv .npmrc .pypirc
  extensions: .pem .key .p12 .pfx .jks .keystore
  exclude_paths: eval/corpus/repos/ tests/ test/ fixtures/ vendor/ build/ dist/
message: "A private key, keystore, or cloud-credential file is committed. Remove it, rotate the credential, add the pattern to .gitignore, and load secrets from a vault/environment. @see KEUR-SECRET-010"
confidence: 0.9
fix: "Remove the file from the repo AND git history (git-filter-repo / BFG), rotate the exposed key/credential immediately (attackers scrape within seconds), and add the pattern to .gitignore (id_rsa, *.pem, *.key, *.p12, credentials.csv). Store secrets in a vault or environment, never in the tree."
